.TH AUTH 6
.SH NAME
auth \- mutual authentication and key exchange
.SH DESCRIPTION
Two parties, each holding a certificate from the same certifying authority (CA),
authenticate each other and agree on keys.
There are two versions of the protocol.
Version 2 is the one
.IR security-auth (2)
speaks unless told otherwise;
version 1 is the original and is described after it.
In both, every message is a four-digit decimal count and a newline,
followed by that many bytes;
an error is
.B !
and a three-digit count and a newline, followed by its text.
.SS "Version 2"
The client is the party that made the connection.
Each first sends its version, the text
.BR 2 ;
the server sends its own only after reading the client's,
and neither continues with a peer that says anything else.
.IP
.EX
C → S  alg, X25519 key || ML-KEM-768 key, CERTc, PKc
S → C  alg, X25519 key || ML-KEM-768 ciphertext, CERTs, PKs,
       sigS("inferno auth 2 server" 0 T), confirmS
C → S  sigC("inferno auth 2 client" 0 T), confirmC
S → C  OK
.EE
.PP
.I Alg
is the protection the client wants for the connection,
.B chacha20poly1305
or
.BR none ;
the server repeats it or refuses.
The key exchange is a hybrid:
the client sends a fresh X25519 public key (32 bytes) and a fresh
ML-KEM-768 encapsulation key (1184 bytes, FIPS 203),
and the server answers with its own fresh X25519 key and
an ML-KEM ciphertext (1088 bytes) for the client's key.
An attacker must break both to learn the result,
so a recording of the exchange made today is not opened
by a later attack on either alone.
.PP
.I T
is the SHA-256 digest of the text
.B "inferno auth 2"
and a zero byte, followed by each of the eight messages before the signatures,
in the order above,
each preceded by a byte
.B c
or
.B s
for its sender and its length as four bytes, most significant first.
Each party checks the other's certificate against the CA's public key
and its expiry time, and then the other's signature of
.I T
under the key that certificate vouches for.
A signature has the form of a certificate
.RI ( keytext (6))
with expiry 0; its digest is
.B sha256
for the algorithms that sign bytes
.RB ( ed25519 ,
.BR ed25519mldsa65 )
and
.B sha1
for the older ones.
Because
.I alg
and both key shares are inside
.IR T ,
changing any of them in transit makes the signatures fail.
.PP
The key material is the 32-byte ML-KEM secret followed by the 32-byte X25519
secret (an all-zero X25519 secret is refused).
Each key is HKDF-SHA256 with
.I T
as the salt, that material as the input, 32 bytes of output, and one of these as the info:
.IP
.EX
inferno auth 2 confirm s
inferno auth 2 confirm c
inferno auth 2 c2s
inferno auth 2 s2c
.EE
.PP
The first two are the confirmation values exchanged above.
The last two are the keys for data from client to server and from server to client,
given to
.IR ssl (3)
with algorithm
.B chacha20poly1305
unless
.I alg
is
.BR none .
.PP
A party that is not satisfied sends an error message in place of its next message.
A client configured for one version never retries with the other,
so an intruder who rewrites the version obtains a failed connection, not an older protocol.
.SS "Version 1"
The following protocol,
based on the Station-to-Station
protocol, is used for mutual authentication of two parties,
each possessing a certificate from the same certifying authority (CA).
.PP
In the description below:
.TF CERTx
.PD
.TP
.I alpha
is a Diffie-Hellman base used system wide
.TP
.I p
is a Diffie-Hellman modulus used system wide
.TP
.I Rx
is a random number of the same order as
.IR p .
.TP
.BI PK x
the public key of
.I x
.TP
.BI SK x
the private key of
.I x
.TP
.BI CERT x
the public key
of
.I x
signed by the certifying authority
.TP
.BI sig n ( x )
represents
.I x
signed with
.IR n 's
private key
.PP
In the following, the parties are labelled 0 and 1.
.PP
Each sends its public key and certificate to the other together with
a computation
.IR "alpha**r0 mod p"
.RI ( "alpha**r1 mod p" )
based on the Diffie-Hellman parameters contained in the certificate:
.IP
.EX
0 → 1  alpha**r0 mod p, CERTu0, PKu0
1 → 0  alpha**r1 mod p, CERTu1, PKu1
.EE
.PP
Each can now use the CA's public key and the certificate received to check
that each has the other's public key.
.PP
Finally, each user signs values known to both that each can then verify:
.IP
.EX
0 → 1  sig0(alpha**r0 mod p, alpha**r1 mod p)
1 → 0  sig1(alpha**r0 mod p, alpha**r1 mod p)
.EE
.PP
At this point 0 and 1 can calculate the shared secret
.BR "alpha**(r0*r1)" ,
and can use it to encrypt later communications.
.PP
Version 1 has no defence against an attacker who can compute discrete logarithms
in the system-wide group, including one who records the exchange now and
computes later, and its choice of algorithm for
.IR ssl (3)
is sent after it, unsigned.
